From ffbdddff99f748cf6c84fb07233d7287be4d26c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:52:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: a 3D display is given the mode of the layout
 that is drawn

While Kodi draws the two eyes of a stereoscopic picture one above the
other or side by side, a display that takes 3D over HDMI was given an
ordinary 2D mode with the two halves in it, and had to be switched to
3D by hand; frame packing, the layout that keeps every line of both
eyes, could not be sent at all.

The GBM window system now has a 3D output for a resolution
(GetStereoOutputMode(), which CGraphicContext asks when it sets one in a
split stereoscopic mode), chosen by STEREO::Choose() from the 3D modes
that the driver lists for the display:

- frame packing when the eyes are drawn one above the other: the 3D mode
  of the timing that Kodi chose or, without a whitelist of modes, the
  largest frame-packed mode of that refresh rate (a 2160p desktop takes
  3D films at 1080p). Kodi draws into one frame of twice the lines and
  the gap, 1920x2205 or 1280x1470;
- else the mode of the half layout that is drawn, top-and-bottom or
  side-by-side;
- else nothing: the 2D mode, as before.

The refresh rate is always the one of the mode that Kodi chose by its
own rules: a film at 24.000 Hz on a display or driver that has frame
packing at 23.976 Hz only gets no frame packing. A mode of another size
is never one of fewer lines than a 3D film has, unless Kodi's own mode
has fewer: the 720p that HDMI has for 50 and 60 Hz is not taken in place
of a 1080p or 2160p mode (what the picture itself has is not known
there).

Asked for side by side, the eyes are to be drawn one above the other
where that gives frame packing (GetStereoOutputArrangement(), for the
commit that asks).

Fail closed:
- by the setting videoscreen.hades3doutput, without atomic modesetting,
  and while native Dolby Vision output has the output, there is none;
- after the commit of a 3D mode the mode that the output is in is read
  back from the kernel. If it does not have the layout of that mode,
  that kind of 3D output (frame packing, or the half modes) is not
  chosen again while the eyes are drawn split, one line at info level
  says so, and the resolution is set once more for the next choice;
- a window for the frame of both eyes that cannot be made is replaced by
  the window of the resolution, the same way, instead of the exception
  that ends Kodi;
- a 3D mode that is still there after a 2D mode was set is set again, up
  to three times.
The log says what the 3D output is whenever that changes ("3D output:").

A mode with a 3D layout is no Dolby Vision transport mode.

For a 2D mode that is set while the output is in no 3D mode and the eyes
are not drawn split, CheckStereoOutput() returns after its first test,
and SetFullScreen() of the OpenGL ES window system makes the window and
sets the mode with the statements it had: the other branch is taken only
when making the window failed and what was to be set has a 3D layout.

DRMStereo.h also gets what nvidia-drm asks of the buffer of a
frame-packed frame, for the commit that follows.

Not covered: the picture is not known where the output is chosen, so a
720p 3D video shown in a larger mode gets no frame packing; frame
packing that the driver refuses after the eyes were turned from side by
side to one above the other leaves them so, in top-and-bottom.

Read and compiled (syntax check of DRMUtils.cpp, WinSystemGbm.cpp,
WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp and WinSystemGbmEGLContext.cpp with the
commands of the last build). The choice has its host test
(drmstereo_test.cpp, 167 checks with the address and undefined behaviour
sanitizers). Nothing of this has run on a GPU or been seen on a 3D
display.
